DC-1 — Penetration Testing Lab Report

Scope: VulnHub DC-1 intentionally vulnerable lab conducted in an authorized local environment.

Reconnaissance

I started by identifying the target machine on my local network using netdiscover.

┌──(kindgod㉿kali)-[~/vulnhub/dc-1]
└─$ sudo netdiscover -r 192.168.1.0/24

The scan identified:

192.168.1.106   08:00:27:ab:cd:ef

The MAC prefix 08:00:27 indicated that the host was running as a VirtualBox virtual machine, matching the expected lab environment.

Target identified: 192.168.1.106

Port and Service Enumeration

Next, I performed a full TCP port scan with service and version detection.

sudo nmap -sS -sV -p- 192.168.1.106

Results:

PORT      STATE SERVICE     VERSION
22/tcp    open  ssh         OpenSSH 6.0p1 Debian
80/tcp    open  http        Apache httpd 2.2.22
111/tcp   open  rpcbind

Three services were exposed:

SSH (22) — Remote administration service.
HTTP (80) — Web server and the most promising initial attack surface.
RPC (111) — Remote Procedure Call service.

The web server became the primary focus for further enumeration.

Enumeration
Web Server Analysis

I began by scanning the web server with Nikto.

nikto -h http://192.168.1.106

The scan revealed:

+ Server: Apache/2.2.22 (Debian)
+ /index.php: Drupal 7.2.3 detected
+ /CHANGELOG.txt: Drupal 7.x detected
+ /robots.txt: Contains 1 entry

The most important finding was the CMS:

Drupal 7.2.3

This was a significantly outdated version of Drupal, making it a potential entry point.

To confirm the CMS and gather additional information, I used Droopescan.

droopescan scan drupal -u http://192.168.1.106

Results:

[+] Drupal version: 7.2.3
[+] Possible interesting urls:
    /CHANGELOG.txt
    /INSTALL.txt
    /README.txt

The version was confirmed.

Vulnerability Research

I searched for publicly known vulnerabilities affecting the identified Drupal version.

searchsploit drupal 7.2

The results included known Drupal remote code execution vulnerabilities.

The important finding was:

Drupalgeddon2 — CVE-2018-7600

This vulnerability affected vulnerable Drupal 7 installations and could allow unauthenticated remote code execution.

Because the lab environment was intentionally vulnerable and authorized for exploitation, I used the appropriate Metasploit module to validate the vulnerability.

Initial Access

I used the Drupalgeddon2 module in Metasploit against the authorized lab target.

The exploitation was successful, resulting in a Meterpreter session.

[*] Meterpreter session 1 opened

I then dropped into a system shell.

meterpreter > shell

Checking the current user showed:

whoami
www-data

Initial access had been achieved through the web service.

However, the www-data account had limited privileges.

I therefore moved to the privilege-escalation phase.

Privilege Escalation
SUID Enumeration

I searched the system for binaries with the SUID permission set.

find / -perm -u=s -type f 2>/dev/null

The results included several binaries, including:

/usr/bin/find
/usr/bin/passwd
/usr/bin/sudo
/usr/sbin/exim4
/bin/su

The most interesting finding was:

/usr/bin/find

The find binary had the SUID bit set.

A SUID binary executes with the permissions of its owner rather than the permissions of the user running it. Since this binary was owned by root, this represented a potential privilege-escalation path within the intentionally vulnerable lab.

I validated the misconfiguration and obtained elevated privileges.

Verification:

whoami
root

Additional verification:

id
uid=33(www-data) gid=33(www-data) euid=0(root)

Although the original user context was www-data, the effective user ID was now root.

Root-level access was successfully achieved.

Flag

With root privileges obtained, I navigated to the root user's directory.

cd /root
ls

The flag was present:

flag.txt

Reading the flag confirmed successful completion of the lab.

cat flag.txt
Congrats! You've rooted DC-1!
